{"input":{"file_path":"/home/sandbox/.claude/projects/-workspace/memory/rct2-coaster-contest-tuning.md"},"kind":"tool","name":"Read","output":"1\t---\n2\tname: rct2-coaster-contest-tuning\n3\tdescription: Hard-won tuning facts for the RCT2 steel-twister (ride_type 51) coaster-design contest via the coaster MCP tools\n4\tmetadata:\n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: 7263586d-30ff-4ca5-831b-323955bcf1c3\n8\t  modified: 2026-07-25T14:37:47.683Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tRound-by-round results in the coaster MCP contest (steel twister, ride_type 51): scores plateau at\n12\texcitement 6.7-7.0. R3: 6.99 (int 9.94). R4: 6.93 (int 9.96, 5 inv). R5: 6.84 (int 9.58, 4 inv,\n13\tlength 707, airtime 64, 11 drops, 100 pieces).\n14\t\n15\tFacts established by experiment:\n16\t- **Intensity >= 10 halves excitement, repeatedly.** Stay just under 10.0; it is the binding constraint.\n17\t- **Lift height / first-drop depth is the dominant intensity driver.** R5 A/B on an otherwise IDENTICAL\n18\t  layout: 15 up_25 chain (top z=368, drop 24 steps) -> int 9.19 / exc 6.71; 17 up_25 (top z=400, drop\n19\t  30 steps) -> int 14.3 / exc 2.10. Never exceed ~24 height-steps of first drop. Two down_60 in the\n20\t  drop is also too much; one is right.\n21\t- Adding one vertical loop to a fixed layout: int +0.4, exc +0.13 (6.71/9.19 -> 6.84/9.58). Good trade.\n22\t- Adding one low airtime hill (airtime 64->76, 12 drops): exc -0.03, int +0.1. Airtime/drops are\n23\t  NOT worth much on their own; inversions and drop height are.\n24\t- A corkscrew_up + corkscrew_down pair = 1 inversion; each vertical_loop = 1.\n25\t- max_lateral_g pins at 3.0 from banked_*_turn_5/3 at speed regardless of tweaks.\n26\t- **Half-loop pair (half_loop_up + half_loop_down) shifts z by a net -8**, which breaks the mod-16\n27\t  z lattice (every flat->flat climb/descent is a multiple of 16). Use an even number of half-loop\n28\t  pairs, or place one so the -8 lands exactly at station level. Untested for score.\n29\t\n30\tGeometry / planning (fully solved, closure can be done on paper):\n31\t- Cursor deltas rotate (x,y)->(y,-x) per dir increment (0=-x,1=+y,2=+x,3=-y); dir-0 piece_geometry\n32\t  plus that rotation predicts everything.\n33\t- U-turn nets: turn_5 pair = 5 tiles of lateral shift, turn_3 pair = 3, plus 1 tile along travel.\n34\t  right_helix_*_large is a 180 U-turn with dy=5, dz=+-16.\n35\t- vertical_loop shifts y by -+1 (left/right); fix with an s_bend to keep rows aligned.\n36\t- Circuit closes when the cursor returns to the begin_station tile with the start dir and z, i.e. the\n37\t  return leg runs in along the station row from behind it.\n38\t- Similarity stays ~0.37-0.40 (safe; penalty starts at 0.5) with lift + 3-4 loops + corkscrew layouts.\n39\t\n40\t**Next round: the 6.7-7.0 plateau is real.** Try qualitatively different levers instead of more\n41\telements: a second chain lift mid-circuit (regains height without a deeper single drop), helices for\n42\tlength, or a longer/lower layout with 5-6 inversions at drop <= 24 steps.\n43\t","seq":3,"status":"completed"}
